# RUN:   -o %t/mult.artifacts > /dev/null
# RUN: %python %S/../SemanticClosure/lib/artifact_handoff.py stage %t/mult.artifacts %t/mult.mlirbc
# RUN: %python %S/../Evidence/Inputs/retarget_request.py %S/Inputs/logup-range-check-request.json %t/mult.artifacts %t/mult.req.json
# RUN: not zkc-derive --signature=%zkc-registry-dir/soundness-signature.json \
# RUN:   --protocol-vocabulary=%t/mult.vocab.json \
# RUN:   --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json \
# RUN:   --request=%t/mult.req.json -o=/dev/null %t/mult.mlirbc 2>&1 \
# RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MULT
# MULT: condition_failed at apply.conditions.S1

# The lemma's own hypothesis. Its proof needs the multiplicities to be
# non-zero as field elements on the lookup side and invertible on the table
# side, and it is stated for sequences of one common length; the unequal case
# reduces to it by padding the shorter side, which leaves the common length at
# the larger of the two. So the condition is on the larger, and a field too
# small for it refuses rather than pricing a protocol the theorem does not
# cover.
# The field is the one the derivation states, not the size of a sampling set:
# a challenge space is how many values are drawn from, and reading it as a
# characteristic would be true only for a prime field sampled whole. That is
# what the two field hypotheses carry, and it is why this leg shrinks the
# space alongside the declared field — a space that does not embed in the
# field is refused first, by a different condition.
# RUN: %python -c "import sys; sys.stdout.write(open(sys.argv[1]).read().replace('space \"2305843009213693951\"','space \"257\"'))" %S/../Encoding/logup-range-check.mlir > %t/tiny.mlir
